{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeMQQTaTHHD8VEPXBjrJ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C6zVRJ5G4P3uMwm6NX3uUa","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/","prompt":"Reproduce the page's prose VERBATIM — do not paraphrase, summarize, or reword. Copy exact sentences. I need, word for word:\n1. The opening definition of init containers (specialized containers that run before app containers in a Pod; init containers can contain utilities or setup scripts not present in an app image).\n2. \"Understanding init containers\" — that a Pod can have multiple containers running apps but can also have one or more init containers that are run before the app containers are started; the list of ways init containers are exactly like regular containers, and the exceptions (they always run to completion; each must complete successfully before the next one starts; they do not support lifecycle, livenessProbe, readinessProbe, or startupProbe because they must run to completion before the Pod can be ready).\n3. \"Differences from regular containers\" and anything about sidecar containers being init containers with restartPolicy Always.\n4. \"Using init containers\" — the full text on how init containers run in order, and what the kubelet does.\n5. \"Detailed behavior\" — the full text: init containers run one at a time in the order specified, each must succeed before the next starts, all must succeed before the Pod's app containers start; what happens if an init container fails (the kubelet repeatedly restarts it according to the Pod's restartPolicy; if restartPolicy is Never and an init container fails during Pod startup, Kubernetes treats the overall Pod as failed); the Pod cannot be Ready until all init containers have succeeded; the Pod status Init: text.\n6. \"Resources\" — the effective init request/limit rules (highest of any particular resource request or limit defined on all init containers is the effective init request/limit; the Pod's effective request/limit is the higher of sum of all app containers and the effective init request/limit).\n7. \"Pod restart reasons\" if present.\nPreserve headings. Exact original wording only."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1928,"cache_read_input_tokens":89685,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1928},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","uuid":"e0cdb195-3f9c-40f4-b273-38a995cdb1c3","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:20:03.631Z","request_id":"req_011CeMQQSYCazYhgHQsPN2dV"}
