{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeMQQTaTHHD8VEPXBjrJ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FkjySTB8pSzbG1zRbmCp5d","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/sidecar-containers/","prompt":"Reproduce VERBATIM — do not paraphrase or summarize. Copy exact sentences. I need, word for word:\n1. The opening definition of sidecar containers (secondary containers that run along with the main application container within the same Pod; these containers are used to enhance or extend the functionality of the primary app container by providing additional services or functionality such as logging, monitoring, security, or data synchronization without directly altering the primary application code).\n2. \"Enabling sidecar containers\" — the statement that Kubernetes implements sidecar containers as a special case of init containers; sidecar containers remain running after Pod startup; that you enable them by setting restartPolicy: Always on an init container entry; the version this became available/stable.\n3. \"Differences from application containers\" / \"Differences from init containers\" — the full comparison text, including that sidecar containers start before app containers, that regular init containers do not support probes but sidecar containers support livenessProbe, readinessProbe and startupProbe, and how sidecar containers affect Pod termination order.\n4. Anything on \"Sidecar containers and Pod lifecycle\" — that if an init container is created with restartPolicy Always, it will start and remain running during the entire life of the Pod; and the ordering of termination.\n5. \"Resource sharing within containers\" if present.\nPreserve headings. Exact original wording only."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1928,"cache_read_input_tokens":