{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"os/)\n\n## Overview\n\nKubernetes classifies the Pods that you run and allocates each Pod into a specific *quality of service (QoS) class*. Kubernetes uses that classification to influence how different pods are handled. K"}},"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"5e59fa08-3f4f-4d06-9c56-7d3e84bba03d"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ubernetes does this classification based on the resource requests of the Containers in that Pod, along with how those requests relate to resource limits. This is known as Quality of Service (QoS) class. Kubernetes assigns every Pod a QoS class based on the resource requests and"}},"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"22c93652-0f50-449a-8ebb-639534e31de9"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" limits of its component Containers. QoS classes are used by Kubernetes to decide which Pods to evict from a Node experiencing Node Pressure. The possible QoS classes are `Guaranteed`, `Burstable`, and `BestEffort`. When a"}},"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"dd7639ba-ca57-4498-9840-3cbef4f3e57f"}
