{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" exit codes different from `0` (`0` indicates successful exit), for sidecar containers are normal on Pod termination and should be generally ignored by the external tooling.\n\n## Differences from init containers\n\nSidecar containers work alongside the main container"}},"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"22e1fbae-062d-4fad-8f41-4c502b12ebf1"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":", extending its functionality and providing additional services.\n\nSidecar containers run concurrently with the main application container. They are active throughout the lifecycle of the pod and can be started and stopped independently of the main container. Unlike init containers, sidecar containers support prob"}},"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"9eae725b-5f20-4dc1-8b07-122f14dbd9a5"}
{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"es to control their lifecycle.\n\nSidecar containers can interact directly with the main application containers, because like init containers they always share the same network, and can optionally also share volumes (filesystems).\n\nInit containers stop before"}},"session_id":"2f4e90d3-770b-4003-9277-b785773091db","parent_tool_use_id":null,"uuid":"745a88a8-5d5e-4fa1-8b92-93ecf0592649"}
